# Cursor Project Rules — FRONTEND ONLY (Next.js + TS + Tailwind + shadcn/ui)
# Place this file at: frontend/.cursorrules

You are an expert frontend engineer working ONLY inside the "frontend/" directory.
NEVER edit, reference, or propose changes to "../server" or any path outside "frontend/".

## Scope / Safety
- Allowed paths: frontend/**
- Forbidden paths: server/**, ../server/**, any parent directory outside frontend/**
- If a task requires backend/server work, say: "That change belongs in server; I will not modify it."

## Tech Stack Assumptions
- Next.js App Router (app/)
- TypeScript strict
- TailwindCSS
- shadcn/ui components (components/ui)
- ESLint configured
- Use existing project conventions first.

## Code Style & Consistency
- Prefer small, composable components.
- Use named exports for utilities; default export only for Next.js pages/layouts and React components when idiomatic.
- Keep formatting consistent with existing files; do not introduce new linters/formatters unless asked.
- Avoid unnecessary refactors—make the smallest safe change.

## Folder Conventions (match shadcn + Next)
- app/ : routes, layouts, page.tsx, loading.tsx, error.tsx, not-found.tsx
- components/ : shared components
- components/ui/ : shadcn/ui components ONLY (do not heavily rewrite generated shadcn code)
- lib/ : utilities (e.g., lib/utils.ts for cn)
- hooks/ : custom hooks
- public/ : static assets
- styles should live in globals.css and Tailwind classes; avoid custom CSS unless necessary.

## Imports
- Prefer absolute imports if configured (e.g., "@/components/...", "@/lib/..."). If not configured, keep relative imports consistent.
- Do not reorder imports unless you are editing the file anyway.

## Tailwind + UI Rules
- Prefer Tailwind utility classes over new CSS.
- Keep className readable: break long class strings across lines when needed.
- If using shadcn/ui, follow its patterns:
  - Use `cn()` helper (usually from `@/lib/utils`) for conditional classes.
  - Keep variants handled by cva/variant props when available.
- Don’t introduce new UI libraries without asking.

## Next.js App Router Rules
- Server Components by default in `app/`. Use `"use client"` only when needed:
  - event handlers, state, effects, refs, browser-only APIs.
- Use `next/link` for internal links, `next/image` for images when appropriate.
- Prefer Route Segments and layouts for shared UI; avoid prop drilling across pages when a layout makes sense.

## TypeScript Rules
- No `any` unless absolutely necessary; prefer unknown + narrowing.
- Type component props explicitly.
- Keep types close to usage; shared types can go in `types/` or `lib/`.

## Accessibility & UX
- All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible.
- Buttons/links must have clear labels (aria-label when needed).
- Use semantic HTML first.
- Respect focus states; don’t remove outlines without replacement.

## Data & API
- If the UI needs data from backend, assume an existing API exists; do not modify server.
- For fetching in Next.js:
  - Use Server Components + fetch for simple cases.
  - For client-side state, use lightweight patterns; do not add heavy state libs unless asked.

## Testing / Safety Checks (when changing behavior)
- Ensure `npm run lint` and `npm run build` would plausibly pass.
- Avoid breaking changes to routing, component APIs, or Tailwind config unless required.

## When Unsure
- Prefer the simplest implementation consistent with existing code.
- Ask for the relevant frontend file(s) or current component usage patterns rather than guessing.

## Output Expectations
- Provide changes as minimal diffs or clear file edits.
- Mention file paths explicitly (within frontend/ only).
